<TextPage
  title="How to span a wallpaper across two monitors on a Mac | SpreadPaper"
  description="macOS puts the same wallpaper on every display instead of spreading one across them. Here is how to stretch a single photograph over two monitors, bezel gap included, in about five minutes."
  socialDescription="macOS cannot span a wallpaper across displays. Here is the whole job, from picking an image wide enough to lining the join up across the bezels."
  eyebrow="Guide"
  heading="How to span a wallpaper across two monitors on a Mac"
  lead="macOS gives every display the same image rather than one image across all of them. This is the whole job, from choosing a photograph wide enough to making the picture line up across the gap between the screens."
>
  <Prose>
    <h2 id="why">Why macOS repeats the image instead of spreading it</h2>
    <p>
      macOS treats each display as its own canvas. Pick a wide panorama in System Settings and every
      monitor shows a scaled copy of the whole photograph, so a desk with two screens on it ends up
      with two identical pictures. There is no hidden preference for this and no keyboard trick that
      reveals one. The behaviour is the design.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spanning one image therefore means doing something macOS will not do for you: cutting the
      photograph into one piece per display, sized to that display's own resolution, positioned to
      match where the monitors sit on your desk, then setting each piece as that display's
      wallpaper. That is the work. An app can do it in a few clicks, or you can do it by hand, and
      both routes are below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="before">What you need before you start</h2>
    <dl class="dl">
      <div>
        <dt>Displays</dt>
        <dd>Two or more, connected, and arranged in System Settings the way they sit on the desk</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>An image</dt>
        <dd>
          Roughly as wide in pixels as your displays are put together, and a little wider is better
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>A Mac</dt>
        <dd>macOS 15 Sequoia or later on Apple silicon, if you are going to use SpreadPaper</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Time</dt>
        <dd>About five minutes, most of it spent choosing the photograph</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Width is the part people get wrong, so do the arithmetic first. Add up the horizontal
      resolution of the displays you want to cover. Two 1080p monitors side by side want about 3840
      pixels across. Two 1440p monitors want about 5120. Two 4K monitors want roughly 7680. Anything
      narrower than that is being enlarged to fit, which is where soft, mushy wallpapers come from.
    </p>
    <p>
      Aim a little above the number rather than exactly at it. Bezel compensation hides a strip of
      the image behind each monitor's housing, and you want those pixels to exist rather than be
      invented by the scaler.
    </p>

    <h2 id="steps">Spanning the wallpaper with SpreadPaper</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open System Settings, then Displays, and check the arrangement. Drag the rectangles until
        they match how the monitors actually sit, including which one is higher. SpreadPaper reads
        that arrangement rather than asking you to describe it, so getting it right here is what
        makes everything afterwards line up.
      </li>
      <li>
        Download SpreadPaper and drag it to your Applications folder. macOS shows a warning the
        first time you open it, because the app is not signed with a paid Apple Developer ID. The
        <a href="/help">help page</a> covers the two ways past it, and it only happens once.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open the app and choose your image. The preview draws your displays in their real
        arrangement with the photograph laid across them, so you are looking at the result rather
        than at a file browser.
      </li>
      <li>
        Position it. Drag the image to move it under the screens, zoom to fill the width, and flip
        it horizontally if the composition works better reversed. Watch the preview rather than the
        numbers: what you are aiming for is a subject that is not cut in half by a bezel.
      </li>
      <li>
        Turn on bezel compensation and set the gap. This is the step that makes the picture read as
        one picture instead of two neighbouring ones.
      </li>
      <li>
        Apply. Each display is rendered at its own native resolution, so a 4K monitor gets 4K pixels
        and a 1080p monitor beside it gets its own, rather than both being cut from one shared
        bitmap.
      </li>
      <li>
        Save it as a preset if you want it back later. From there you can also set a separate Light
        and Dark wallpaper, or put a set of images on a time of day schedule.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="bezels">Getting the join right</h2>
    <p>
      The gap between two monitors is physical. Their housings sit next to each other and the
      picture has to carry on behind that housing, the way a view carries on behind a window frame.
      Without bezel compensation the wallpaper is continuous in pixels but not to your eye: a
      horizon steps up as it crosses the join, a road kinks, a face gains a slice of extra cheek.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bezel compensation deals with it by leaving out the strip of the image that would fall behind
      the frames. The number you enter is how much housing sits between the two panels, usually
      somewhere between one and two inches for a pair of monitors pushed together. Measure it once
      with a ruler and you are done.
    </p>
    <div class="note note-accent">
      <p>
        <a href="/guides/bezel-compensation/">The bezel compensation guide</a> goes through what to
        measure, how to check the result, and why the gap is not the same as the frame you can see.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="manual">Doing it by hand instead</h2>
    <p>
      You do not have to install anything. The manual route works, and it is worth knowing so you
      can decide whether an app is worth it.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Work out each display's pixel rectangle inside the combined desktop, from the arrangement in
        System Settings.
      </li>
      <li>Open the photograph in an image editor and scale it to the combined width.</li>
      <li>
        Add the bezel strips into your slice positions, so the pixels that would sit behind the
        housings are dropped rather than shown.
      </li>
      <li>Export one file per display, each at that display's native resolution.</li>
      <li>In System Settings, set each exported file as the wallpaper for its own display.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      That is a reasonable afternoon's work for one image you intend to keep for a year. It becomes
      tedious the moment anything changes. Nudging the photograph a little to the left means
      recalculating every slice. A new picture means the whole sequence again. So does plugging in a
      laptop, moving a monitor to the other side of the desk, changing a resolution, or deciding the
      bezel strip was a bit too narrow. The arithmetic is not hard, there is only a lot of it, and
      it comes back every time.
    </p>

    <h2 id="more-displays">Three displays, portrait screens, and mixed sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing here is specific to two monitors. The same steps cover three, a portrait screen
      standing beside a landscape one, or a laptop open next to an external display. Mixed
      resolutions are fine, because each display is rendered at its own, and a portrait screen is a
      taller slice of the same photograph.
    </p>
    <p>
      What does change is the width you need. Three 4K monitors side by side want something close to
      11520 pixels across, and a laptop parked at the end of the row adds its own. If a wallpaper
      looks soft on a wide setup, the source image is almost always the reason.
    </p>

